WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.410
A student's course database

2
00:00:02.410 --> 00:00:04.510
keeps the lecturer's phone

3
00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:07.130
number on every enrolment row.

4
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.720
The lecturer changes phones, half the

5
00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.080
rows are updated, and now the

6
00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.320
database disagrees with itself.

7
00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:18.770
Why did the design allow that?

8
00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:23.320
Today: Codd's relational model, how

9
00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:25.000
functional dependencies drive

10
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.900
normalisation, and how to teach design

11
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.500
so students stop building one giant table.

12
00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:34.480
Codd proposed storing data as relations:

13
00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.280
sets of tuples over named attributes.

14
00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:40.730
A key identifies each tuple uniquely.

15
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.460
Queries describe what you

16
00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:45.600
want, not how to fetch it.

17
00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:49.150
A functional dependency says one set of

18
00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:50.790
attributes determines another.

19
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.060
Lecturer determines phone.

20
00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.100
Student and course

21
00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:56.840
together determine grade.

22
00:00:57.530 --> 00:00:59.770
These dependencies, not intuition,

23
00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:01.870
tell us how to split tables.

24
00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.950
Phone depends on lecturer, which depends

25
00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:08.250
on course, not on the whole key.

26
00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:10.390
That transitive dependency

27
00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:12.090
breaks third normal form.

28
00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:15.290
Moving lecturer details to their own

29
00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:17.530
table removes the update anomaly.

30
00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:21.220
Third normal form and Boyce–Codd

31
00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:23.680
normal form remove most redundancy

32
00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:25.400
while keeping joins lossless.

33
00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:27.840
Analytical systems sometimes

34
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.020
denormalise on purpose for speed.

35
00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.360
Students should know why, not just how.

36
00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:36.390
Students memorise normal-form

37
00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:38.470
definitions without seeing the point.

38
00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:42.560
Start instead with a broken table and ask

39
00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:44.920
them to insert, update and delete.

40
00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:48.560
Once they hit the anomalies, dependencies

41
00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.600
become the tool that fixes them.

42
00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:52.350
Quick check!

43
00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:56.380
A new lecturer cannot be stored until they

44
00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:58.800
have at least one enrolled student.

45
00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:01.400
Which anomaly is that?

46
00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:04.010
An insertion anomaly.

47
00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:06.560
Facts about lecturers are

48
00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:08.480
trapped inside enrolment rows.

49
00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.080
A separate lecturer table lets us

50
00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:12.080
record them independently.

51
00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:16.080
In the app, open the schema lab.

52
00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:20.540
Split a flat table into related tables and

53
00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:23.280
check that every fact is stored once.

54
00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:25.160
Your turn.

55
00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:29.330
A table holds course, lecturer and

56
00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:31.970
lecturer's office, keyed by course.

57
00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.400
Is it in third normal form?

58
00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:38.070
No: office depends on lecturer,

59
00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:40.890
a non-key attribute, so the

60
00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:42.850
dependency is transitive.

61
00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:45.360
Remember: keys identify,

62
00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:48.640
dependencies decide the split, and

63
00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.760
anomalies motivate the theory.

64
00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:54.210
Try it in your next lecture: give

65
00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:56.090
groups a flat enrolment spreadsheet.

66
00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:58.990
Ask each group to perform one

67
00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:02.070
insert, one update and one delete

68
00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.030
and report what went wrong.

69
00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.000
Then derive the dependencies together.
